NAME
       diff - show how two files (or directories) differ

SYNOPSIS
       diff [-u] [-q] [-r] [-i] [-w] [-y] FILE1 FILE2

DESCRIPTION
       diff compares two text files line by line and prints what would turn
       FILE1 into FILE2. In the default format, lines starting with < come
       from FILE1 and lines with > from FILE2. The unified format (-u) is
       easier to read: - marks a removed line, + an added one, and a few
       lines around each change are shown for context.

       The exit status is 0 when the files are the same, 1 when they differ
       and 2 on trouble.

OPTIONS
       -u, --unified
              The unified format.

       -q, --brief
              Only say whether the files differ.

       -r, --recursive
              Compare two directories and everything in them.

       -i, --ignore-case
              Treat upper and lower case as equal.

       -w, --ignore-all-space
              Ignore all white space.

       -y, --side-by-side
              Show the two files in two columns.

EXAMPLES
       See what you changed in a configuration file:

           cp /etc/ssh/sshd_config /root/sshd_config.orig
           vi /etc/ssh/sshd_config
           diff -u /root/sshd_config.orig /etc/ssh/sshd_config

       Compare two directory trees:

           diff -rq /etc /root/etc-backup

SEE ALSO
       cmp(1), comm(1), patch(1)
